readings.c1s1:
¶
{{smaller block|In tale or history your beggar is ever the just ''antipode'' to your king.
{{right|''Lamb'', Decay of Beggars.}}
<poem>Balance-loving Nature
Made all things in pairs,
To every foot its ''antipode''.</poem>
{{right|''Emerson'', Merlin, ii.}}}}

'''antipodean''' (an-tip-ọ̄-dē′a̤n), ''a.'' Pertaining to the antipodes; antipodal.

'''antipodes''' (an-tip′ọ̄-dēz), ''n. pl.'' [L. (in ME. as L.), < Gr. ἀντίποδες, pl. of ἀντίπους, with feet opposite, < ἀντί, opposite, + πούς, pl. πόδες, = E. ''foot''.] '''1.''' Persons living at diametrically opposite points of the globe, so that their feet are directed toward each other; persons who live on the side of the globe opposite to others.
{{smaller block|Your ''Antipodes'' are a good rascally sort of topsie turvy Fellows — If I had a Bumper I’d stand upon my Head and drink a Health to ’em. ''Congreve'', Way of the World, iv. 10.}}
'''2.''' Two places on the surface of the globe diametrically opposite to each other; the country or region on the opposite side of the globe.— '''3.''' Figuratively, things opposed to each other: as a singular, anything diametrically adverse or opposed to another thing belonging to the same general order; a contrary. In the latter sense sometimes used in the singular form ''antipode'' (which see).
{{smaller block|Can there be a greater contrariety unto Christ’s judgment, a more perfect ''antipodes'' to all that hath hitherto been gospel?
{{right|''Hammond'', Sermons.}}
Minds, the ''antipodes'' of each other in temper and endowment, alike feel the force of his [Dante’s] attraction.
{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 2d ser., p. 39.}}}}

'''antipodic''' (an-ti-pod′ik), ''a.'' Same as ''antipodal''. ''Ruskin.''

readings.c2s3:
or collector of antiquities: sometimes used in the sense of ''archæologist.'' See ''antiquarianism.''
{{smaller block|<poem>With sharpen’d sight pale ''antiquaries'' pore,
Th’ inscription value, but the rust adore.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Ep. to Addison, l. 35.}}}}
{{smaller block|The simple ''antiquary'' is not a historian, but it is always a gain when the historian is an ''antiquary.''
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Amer. Lects., p. 208.}}}}
'''2.''' A dealer in old books, coins, objects of art, and similar articles. In this and the preceding sense also ''antiquarian.''—'''3†.''' An official custodian of antiquities. {{smaller|This title was bestowed by Henry VIII. upon Leland, his chaplain and librarian, 1533.}}

'''antiquate''' (an′ti-kwāt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''antiquated'', ppr. ''antiquating.'' [< L. ''antiquatus'', pp. of ''antiquare'', restore to its ancient condition, in LL. make old, < ''antiquus'', ancient: see ''antique.''] To make old or obsolete; make old and useless by substituting something newer and better.
{{smaller block|The growth of Christianity . . . might reasonably introduce new laws and ''antiquate'' or abrogate some old ones.
{{right|''Sir M. Hale'', Hist. Common Law of Eng.}}}}
{{smaller block|Huge charts which subsequent discoveries have ''antiquated.''
{{right|''Lamb'', Elia, p. 9.}}}}

'''antiquate''' (an′ti-kwāt), ''a.'' Same as ''antiquated'', ''p. a.''

'''antiquated''' (an′ti-kwā-ted), ''p. a.'' '''1.''' Grown old; obsolete or obsolescent; ill adapted to present use; old-fashioned: said of things: as, an ''antiquated'' law.
{{smaller block|Is it possible that the present age can be pleased with that ''antiquated'' dialect?
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xviii.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
and Rome: as, an ''antique'' statue.
{{smaller block|The seals . . . which we know to be ''antique''. {{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>My copper-lamps, at any rate,
For being true ''antique'', I bought.</poem>
{{right|''Prior'', Alma, iii.}}}}
'''2.''' Belonging to former times, as contrasted with modern; having the form and characteristics of an earlier day; of old fashion: as, an ''antique'' robe.
{{smaller block|<poem>O good old man; how well in thee appears
The constant service of the ''antique'' world,
When service sweat for duty, not for meed!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|All the ''antique'' fashions of the street were dear to him; even such as were characterized by a rudeness that would naturally have annoyed his fastidious senses.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, xi.}}}}
'''3†.''' Fantastic; fanciful; odd; wild; antic. See ''antic'', 4.
{{smaller block|<poem>What fashion’d hats, or ruffs, or suits next year
Our giddy-headed ''antique'' youth will wear. {{right|''Donne.''}}</poem>}}
'''4.''' In ''bookbinding'', embossed without gold.—{{smaller|'''Antique crown''', in ''her.'', a bearing representing a simple crown composed of a circular band with rays simply
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Antique Crown.<br>(From Berry’s “Dict. of Heraldry.”)}}}}
pointed and of indefinite number. It is always ''or'', that is, of gold. Also called ''Eastern crown''.—'''Antique type.''' See II., 3.='''Syn.''' ''Ancient'', ''Old'', ''Antique'', etc. See ''ancient''.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' The style or manner of ancient times, specifically of Greek and Roman antiquity: used especially of art. {{smaller|In this sense used only in the singular, and preceded by the definite article: as, fond of ''the antique''; copied from}}
